==Status Report: 2026-10-24==
Root cause (primary): WcastMediaService.onAudioFocusChanged() treated every
AUDIOFOCUS_LOSS_TRANSIENT as "we were interrupted mid-playback" and set
pausedByFocusLoss=true so the next AUDIOFOCUS_GAIN auto-resumed. But on a manual
pause Wcast keeps its audio focus, so when a phone call arrives while the user is
*already paused* the service still receives LOSS_TRANSIENT and then GAINs on
hangup, restarting speech the user had deliberately left paused. The log shows
exactly this at 11:22:23 (focus change=-2, already PAUSED) -> 11:22:30 (focus
change=1) -> resume.

Fix: on LOSS_TRANSIENT, only schedule an auto-resume if speech was actually
playing at that moment (SpeechControllerBridge.isPlaying). If the app was already
paused, the flag stays false and the user's pause survives the call.

    AudioManager.AUDIOFOCUS_LOSS_TRANSIENT -> {
        hasFocus = false
        pausedByFocusLoss = SpeechControllerBridge.isPlaying
        if (pausedByFocusLoss) SpeechControllerBridge.onPause?.invoke()
    }

Secondary (Bluetooth media button not pausing): the attached log shows media
buttons working normally after this session (e.g. 11:22:39
handleSetPlayWhenReady=false status=PLAYING pauses fine). The reported "button did
nothing" stretch is most consistent with the AVRCP session not cleanly
re-establishing after the phone call (a system/Bluetooth-side transition we can't
log or fully control), and it surfaced alongside the confusing auto-resume above.
Fixing the auto-resume removes the state Wcast was in when the button misbehaved,
so I'm leaving the Bluetooth half as a watch-item rather than claiming a code fix.

